Medial Epicondylitis (Golfer's Elbow)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Medial epicondyle localized pain, Pain exacerbated by resisted wrist flexion, Tenderness at common flexor origin

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-031
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe medial elbow pain causing sudden, complete loss of grip strength and inability to perform essential manual tasks.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing agonizing medial elbow pain and sudden, profound weakness in hand grasp following intense manual labor. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for acute medial collateral ligament rupture or ulnar nerve entrapment, and to provide immediate mechanical offloading and potent analgesia to restore basic functional safety and prevent further structural damage prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The inside of my elbow is throbbing intensely, and when I try to grip anything, a sharp pain shoots down my arm and my hand just gives way."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute-on-chronic exacerbation of right medial elbow pain. Timeline: Insidious onset of a deep ache localized to the medial epicondyle over the past three weeks; rapidly escalated 8 hours ago following a prolonged period of forceful, repetitive wrist flexion and pronation while using heavy hand tools. Currently reports severe, aching pain radiating distally along the flexor-pronator muscle mass of the volar forearm. Patient experiences severe exacerbation during any attempt at a strong hand grasp. Reports occasional mild numbness radiating into the ulnar digits.
Functional Status: Significant functional impairment of the dominant upper limb; unable to safely lift or grasp objects due to pain inhibition.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of dropping objects; unable to perform basic domestic duties.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 136/84 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate distress, supporting the right forearm with the contralateral hand.
Physical Exam: Right elbow reveals exquisite point tenderness located directly on and just distal to the medial epicondyle at the common flexor origin. Pain is strongly and distinctly reproduced during resisted active wrist flexion with the elbow fully extended. Discomfort is severely provoked by passive stretching of the wrist into full extension and supination. Pronounced grip weakness noted on dynamometry, clinically secondary to pain inhibition rather than primary motor deficit. Tinel's sign at the cubital tunnel is mildly positive, consistent with secondary local ulnar nerve irritation. No joint effusion or erythema. Valgus stress testing is stable.
Environmental Observations: Heavy hand tools present in the living area, corroborating the mechanism of repetitive flexor overload.
Medication Reconciliation: Took Ibuprofen 400mg 4 hours ago with minimal relief of the acute flare.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute flare of Medial Epicondylitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ulnar Neuropathy (Cubital Tunnel Syndrome) - Ruled out as the primary cause due to the focal mechanical pain on resisted wrist flexion and maximal tenderness at the epicondyle, though mild secondary nerve irritation is present.

Medial Collateral Ligament Sprain - Ruled out by a stable and painless valgus stress test of the elbow joint.

Cervical Radiculopathy (C8/T1) - Ruled out by completely normal deep tendon reflexes, negative Spurling's test, and preservation of intrinsic hand muscle strength.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, functionally limiting exacerbation of medial epicondylitis following a significant mechanical overload event. The sudden loss of grip strength secondary to pain inhibition rendered the dominant limb functionally useless, posing a safety risk. Delaying evaluation until regular clinic hours would have subjected the patient to unmanaged severe pain, potential thermal or mechanical injury from dropped objects, and further micro-tearing of the inflamed tendon origin. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed the localized tendinopathic nature of the pain, ruled out structural ligamentous rupture or primary cervical radiculopathy, and permitted the immediate application of mechanical splinting and targeted analgesia.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Naproxen 500mg BD with food for 7 days to aggressively manage acute tendinous inflammation.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Strictly lift all objects with the palm facing downward (pronated) for the next 7 days. Consequence: Complete mechanical bypassing of the damaged flexor tendons, allowing the patient to lift lightweight items securely without triggering the sharp, yielding pain (negative reinforcement).
Action: Apply an ice pack directly to the bony bump on the inside of the elbow for 15 minutes after any mandatory hand use. Consequence: Rapid constriction of the inflamed tissue, providing immediate, soothing numbness and reducing the deep, throbbing ache (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral for eccentric flexor strengthening protocol once the acute pain subsides.
DME/Safety (Optional): Fitted a counterforce elbow brace positioned 2 cm distal to the medial epicondyle.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2-3 weeks to monitor functional recovery.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the elbow becomes diffusely swollen, hot, and red, if a high fever develops, or if there is a sudden, total numbness or severe weakness in the ring and little fingers.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, reflecting tendon micro-tearing and inflammation. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute, sharp pain will improve over 1-2 weeks with the brace and modified lifting, but the underlying tendon healing requires months of dedicated rehabilitation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the entire elbow becomes hot, red, and swollen, or if you completely lose feeling in your pinky and ring fingers.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tendons that curl your wrist inward have been severely strained where they attach to the inside of your elbow. The counterforce brace acts like an artificial anchor, spreading the pulling force away from the damaged area. The anti-inflammatories will quiet the chemical pain signals.
Actionable Steps: Do not lift anything with your palm facing up. Always lift overhand (palm down); this uses the extensors, giving the damaged flexors a complete rest. Wear the brace firmly during any gripping tasks.

